              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Assemble the question — your + name + what-is. And notice the copula hiding in the last word."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "unknown",
          "title": "The phrase, assembled",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "നിന്റെ (ninṟe, \"your\") = the possessive of nī (\"you\")."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "എന്താണ് (entāṇŭ) = entŭ (what) + āṇŭ (is), fused into one."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "നിന്റെ പേര് (pēr) എന്താണ്? = literally \"your name what is?\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Unlike the Tamil question (which had no verb), Malayalam carries its copula āṇŭ right inside the question-word entāṇŭ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: answer, and the respectful version",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Answer with the sentence you built: Enṟe pēru Arun āṇŭ. The respectful \"your\" is niṅṅaḷuṭe: niṅṅaḷuṭe pēru entāṇŭ?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
