              "rowCount": 4,
              "utterances": [
                "എന്റെ പേര് മീരാ ആണ്. (enṟe pēru Mira āṇŭ) means My name is Mira.",
                "നിന്റെ പേര് എന്താണ്? (ninṟe pēru entāṇŭ) means What's your name?",
                "എന്റെ പേര് അരുൺ ആണ്. (enṟe pēru Arun āṇŭ) means My name is Arun.",
                "സന്തോഷം. (santōṣam) means Pleased to meet you."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Every atom traced:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pēru from Dravidian pēr (not name; twin of Tamil peyar)."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "enṟe from ñāṉ (\"I\")."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "āṇŭ from the verb āka (\"to be\") — Malayalam's copula."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "entŭ from Dravidian question-stem yā-/e-."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "santōṣam from Sanskrit (borrowed even in native-loving Malayalam)."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The Malayalam standout: unlike its Dravidian sisters' zero copula, Malayalam has a word for \"is\" (āṇŭ) — a real grammatical difference between the closest of sisters."
            }
          ]
        },
